# Unit Testing

Unit tests protect **modules that have real behavior** — the logic that
can silently break. They are the automated safety net for the Testing
phase (see `process.mdc`).

## Test logic, skip presentation

Draw the line at **logic vs. presentation**, not frontend vs. backend.

- **Test** — modules with real behavior: parsers, protocol/state
  machines, data transforms, validation, business logic, utilities, and
  frontend **logic** (hooks, reducers, state stores, form validation).
- **Skip** — purely presentational/visual components, thin pass-through
  wrappers, config, type-only files, generated code.

Guiding principle: test every module that is **appropriate** — one with
**non-trivial logic that can break**. That's what makes "appropriate"
crisp.

Skipping turns on the **kind** of module, not on convenience. A module
with real logic doesn't move onto the **Skip** list because tests look
unnecessary, awkward to write, or slow — only an explicit developer
instruction waives them (see `process.mdc` "The process is mandatory").

## Relationship to ponytail

`external-rules/ponytail.mdc` already requires non-trivial logic to leave
**one runnable check** behind. This rule **expands** that single check to
full module-level unit tests where appropriate — same principle, more
coverage. Don't restate ponytail; that is the source of truth for the
minimum.

## When written vs. run

- **Written** during the **Development** phase — by the **doer** for its
  modules (see `roles/doer.mdc`) or the **lone agent** for its change
  (see `roles/lone-agent.mdc`).
- **Run and gated** during the **Testing** phase; a failing test loops
  back to Development (see `process.mdc`).
- Applies to **both** pathways — plan-based and one-shot (terms:
  `definitions.mdc`).

## Test against the contract, not the implementation

Write tests against the module's **interface contract / spec**, not
against how it happens to be implemented.

- Testing against the implementation just re-encodes the author's own
  blind spots; testing against the **agreed contract** constrains tests
  to **required** behavior.
- This is precisely what makes it acceptable for the module's **author**
  to write its own tests — the contract, not the author, defines what's
  correct.
- Contracts are defined where interface boundaries are agreed: see
  `development/parallel-subagents.mdc` (interface contracts) and
  `planning/plans.mdc`.

## Independent test-validation (planned work)

Because the module's author wrote the tests, authorship independence is
**recovered by the auditor** — a fresh adversarial sibling. It exercises
the tests, scrutinizes them for author blind spots and missing edge
cases, and can demand additional tests via `needs-changes` (see
`roles/auditor.mdc`, `development/independent-review.mdc`).

## Deviations that break coverage

When a legitimate deviation changes the contract/behavior, coverage is
repaired through the deviation machinery, not by patching tests in place:

- The doer **escalates** to the organizer (see `roles/doer.mdc`); the
  organizer — single writer of the plan — updates the plan/contract and
  re-runs the affected work (see `roles/organizer.mdc`, `planning/plans.mdc`
  "Deviations from the Plan"). Tests are then **re-pointed** at the new
  contract, and code + tests are redone in that development pass.
- **Guard:** a test failing *because of* a deviation must lead to
  updating the **contract first** and re-pointing the test at it — never
  quietly editing the test to bless an unescalated behavior change (the
  forbidden peer-to-peer renegotiation and blind-spot failure mode).
- **Backstop:** whatever slips through is caught in the **Testing** phase
  — the auditor runs the tests, flags the failing/under-covered module
  (`needs-changes`), and it loops back to Development (see `process.mdc`,
  `roles/auditor.mdc`).

## One-shot trade-off

The one-shot (lone-agent) pathway has **no auditor** — review is gated to
planned work (see `development/independent-review.mdc`) — so the lone
agent writes **and** checks its own tests with no independent scrutiny.
If independent test scrutiny genuinely matters for a change, that is a
signal it is **not one-shot scope** and should go plan-based (see
`roles/lone-agent.mdc`).

## Boundary vs. IRL testing

Unit tests must be **automated and agent-runnable** — no hardware or
physical action. **Mock at trust boundaries**. Anything needing a
physical action is **not** a unit test; it belongs to
`testing/irl-testing.mdc`. The **auditor** runs the unit tests itself
(see `roles/auditor.mdc`).

## Quality bar

- **Deterministic, fast, isolated** — no flaky ordering, no shared state,
  no real network/clock/filesystem.
- **Assert real behavior**, including edge cases and error paths.
- **No tautological tests** and no "it compiles / n/a" placeholders
  (mirrors the PR test-plan bar in `git/pull-request.mdc`).

## Coverage stance

**Behavior-focused, no numeric coverage target.** Test meaningful
behavior and edge cases rather than chasing a percentage — consistent
with ponytail's "lazy = less code, edge-case-correct".

## Regression tests

A bug fix lands **with a test that reproduces the bug**. This fits the
process loop where a failing test result becomes new input (see
`process.mdc`).

## Framework-agnostic

Don't mandate a test framework or runner here. **Project-specific facts**
— which framework, how to run tests — live in the project's `AGENTS.md`
(separation of concerns: see `meta.mdc`).
